From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: appxdeploymentclient: frameworks, updates, resource packages, aliases, winget's calls

What patches 0060/0063 left out of deployment, and what winget needed to
install and uninstall MSIX packages:

- Frameworks and dependencies: the packages passed with AddPackageAsync
  are deployed first; every PackageDependency must then be met by an
  installed package (name, publisher, MinVersion, an architecture the
  package can load) or nothing is installed
  (ERROR_INSTALL_RESOLVE_DEPENDENCY_FAILED, naming the framework as
  Windows does). The full names that met them are the record's
  Dependencies (Package.Dependencies; ntdll's package graph). A framework
  a package depends on cannot be removed (ERROR_REMOVE_FAILED).
- Versions: a newer main package replaces the older one; an older one is
  refused (ERROR_INSTALL_PACKAGE_DOWNGRADE) unless
  ForceUpdateFromAnyVersion. Frameworks live side by side.
- Bundles: the application package for this machine plus the resource
  packages for the user's languages (GetUserPreferredUILanguages) and the
  scale nearest 100, each registered as a resource package and removed
  with the application.
- App execution aliases (wine/appexeclink.h): a file per alias in
  WindowsApps and in its family's folder there; the folder is added to the
  user's PATH (as Windows has it from the start). Removal takes only
  aliases still pointing into the package.
- http/https package locations are downloaded first.
- IPackageManager6 (winget uses RequestAddPackageAsync),
  RemovePackageWithOptionsAsync, FindPackages*WithPackageTypes by type
  (queries without types list main and framework packages),
  FindPackagesByPackageFamilyNameWithPackageTypes; Package:
  Dependencies, InstalledDate, IsResourcePackage and IPackage4
  (SignatureKind: winget's installed-package list asks for it). The
  IIDs come from the MIT-licensed windows crate (Microsoft's metadata).

